From: Emba <hello@emba.au>

ERRSTS lives in the host bridge and is shared by all memory
controllers, and writing it clears the controller error logs. The
driver reads and clears one MCI at a time, so handling mc0 can
discard an error that mc1 has latched but not yet reported. The MSR
clear path has the same ordering problem since its clear also acts
on the whole package.

Snapshot the error logs of every configured IMC and channel first,
then clear once. If the PCI status changes while the snapshot is
being taken, redo it so a late error is not half-captured, and
report the shared transition once. Each captured log is dispatched
to the MCI that owns it.

All polling callbacks are kept so EDAC keeps reporting every MCI as
polled; the ordered EDAC workqueue serializes the package checks and
an already-cleared ERRSTS makes the second callback a no-op. Checks
are gated until every MCI is registered and the initial clear has
run, and teardown stops all workers before the shared package state
is unmapped.

